     </draw:image>
    </draw:frame><text:span text:style-name="T4">Eclipse Identity Recognition Corporation </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T6">TM</text:span> provides <text:span text:style-name="T8">Personal Identity Recognition</text:span> via its face-based analytics known as <text:span text:style-name="T9">INDIface </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T6">TM</text:span>. It is used to detect faces and facial features and then generate face templates for searching and matching. In addition, the <text:span text:style-name="T9">INDIface</text:span> analytics can also determine skin tone, upper and lower clothes color, and an estimated height of a person. The <text:span text:style-name="T9">INDIbase </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T6">TM</text:span> system provides for the enrollment, storage, management, retrieval, and matching of <text:span text:style-name="T9">INDIface</text:span> templates. <text:span text:style-name="T9">INDIface</text:span> also manages facial and other images as well as personal identification and characteristic data.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">The “Road to <text:span text:style-name="T12">INDI</text:span> (Integrated Network Digital Imaging)” began in January 2002 by recognizing that a convergence of automatic facial recognition, digital surveillance video and archiving, and access control was taking place. This in conjunction with realization that digital processing, communication, and storage capabilities were increasing while becoming more affordable. The concept was to use these trends to accomplish things in digital media that were previously unavailable on the desktop. Application sectors included transportation safety, gaming, retail, financial, corporate, law enforcement, and utilities.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T16">In EclipseIR&apos;s initial generation, </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4">FusionIR</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T6">TM</text:span>, the <text:span text:style-name="T9">INDIface</text:span> Software Development Kit (SDK1), allows third-party developers to create custom applications using EclipseIR&apos;s technology. It runs on Microsoft Windows-based systems as a stand-alone console application with a capacity of over 50,000 enrollments. EclipseIR&apos;s own applications use exactly the same SDK available to OEM developers.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">While SDK1 is essentially single-threaded, the second generation of <text:span text:style-name="T9">INDIface</text:span> (<text:span text:style-name="T15">If</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7">2</text:span>) will focus on multiple threads to split the work across multicore, multiprocessor servers, and even, multiple-server (i.e. blade) systems. <text:span text:style-name="T15">If</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7">2</text:span> will introduce an SQL-based <text:span text:style-name="T9">INDIbase</text:span> enrollment repository and a plug-in Frame Source Architecture and an Image Manager Service to accept video channels from a wider variety of sources.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11">In the second generation, FusionIR will be wrapped in two architectures. The second generation software development kit (SDK2) will mimic the interface of SDK1. An application program interface (API2) will be based upon messages passed via TCP/IP sockets; the command, control, and configuration packets can be between applications on a single system or across a wide area network. Both SDK2 and API2 can be implemented as Windows services and &apos;BSD/Linux daemons. The API2 will serve as the basis for interfacing with other standard APIs, such as BioAPI and the Onvif SOAP standard for video analytics.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T13">This document will </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T14">....</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P39" text:outline-level="1">Overview</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19">INDIface, EclipseIR Applicaitons, and Other Intellectual Property</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T13">This section will </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T14">...</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P37" text:outline-level="2">Core Technology</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19">The INDIface system</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">This chapter will...</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">The basic operations of <text:span text:style-name="T9">INDIface</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T6"> </text:span>can be broken into five functional blocks in addition to the separate processes for <text:span text:style-name="T5">INDIbase</text:span> and Image Manager Service SQL interfaces: Frame Processing, Face Processing, Analytic Processing, Template Processing, and Result Fusion.</text:p>
